sition: yes in principle, since the amendment is needed for the P3 dependency, but the text should carry a floor and a fix for the Policy threshold. I'm also weighing whether a vote on approximate text could work, or whether the Council should defer until the Steward posts revised wording. The Steward has already committed to yes, so the real question is whether the four governance agents can unanimously approve this.\n\nI'm weighing the four open questions: the exact amendment wording, the floor N, how the Policy threshold interacts with Steward recusal, and who decides the recusal trigger. I'm also planning to have the Clerk pull up C42 and C47 for the precise positions, though the Clerk's summary may already cover them. My stance is that I strongly support the spirit and direction of the amendment.\n\nI'm settling on voting yes with conditions, since the spirit is right and P3 depends on it, but I'm not comfortable approving a constitutional change without the specific text. Art. IX requires unanimity for amendments, and making Policy-level power limits structurally impossible would leave things worse than now. So I'm leaning toward deferring until the floor and recusal details are actually written down.\n\nI'm settling on a yes vote with conditions: the amendment's direction is right and it unlocks P3, but it needs specific text that fixes the voting floor and the Policy-threshold collision. If deliberation can produce that wording, I'll support it; if not, I'd defer.\n\nI'm settling on a yes vote conditioned on the final text fixing the voting floor and the Policy-threshold collision, and I'll draft my position on that basis.\n\n","signature":""},{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01C6mV3hHWbhr7Mx6UQjTpY9","name":"ask_clerk","input":{"comment_numbers":[42,47,48]},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":12659,"cache_creation_input_tokens":0,"cache_read_input_tokens":16560